class LobbySettings(GameModel):
    model_config = ConfigDict(
        extra="ignore",
    )
    map: MapTemplate | None = None
    """
    Start on this map file. Give this or map_template_id, not both.
    """
    map_template_id: Annotated[StrictInt | None, Field(ge=1)] = None
    """
    Start on this default map, a map_template_id from GET /map/templates. Give this or map, not both.
    """
    max_generations: Annotated[StrictInt | None, Field(ge=50, le=1200)] = 300
    """
    The most turns the game may last, a whole number from 50 to 1200; 300 when left out. The turn that creates generation max_generations ends the game, if nothing has ended it before, and the team with the most castles wins.
    """
    turn_pace_milliseconds: Annotated[StrictInt | None, Field(ge=500, le=5000)] = 1000
    """
    How long each turn lasts in a real-time game, in whole milliseconds from 500 to 5000; 1000 when left out. A game is real-time when a player on its roster plays by hand: each turn then ends this long after its generation was created, and the server plays it with the commands that arrived, so a player who sent nothing passes. A game of bots only doesn't use it and waits for every player.
    """
    turn_timeout_seconds: Annotated[StrictInt | None, Field(ge=5, le=600)] = 30
    """
    How long each turn lasts, in whole seconds from 5 to 600, counted from when its generation was created; 30 when left out. null means no limit, for example while debugging a bot. When a turn's deadline passes before every player has completed it, each team with a player who hadn't loses, and the game ends. In a real-time game, see turn_pace_milliseconds, no turn times out; instead a player the server hasn't heard from for this long, or for one turn plus 5 seconds when that is longer, by the end of a turn has left the game and their team is out, and a game that no request reaches for this long after its open turn's end is abandoned: the team with the most castles wins. null turns both rules off.
    """


